														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4214357">
									

<p>This is a hard one for me. Prose-wise, it's strong; you write clear, tight sentences. The interviews are solid, and the article is relatively well-paced and structured (though I think the first addendum with all the evidence is probably unnecessary).</p>
<p>Otherwise?</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">THOUGHTS</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>It's not just that it's an X-Man skip; the moment you hit the &quot;Jerry was his name. Jerry Siegal&quot; bit, I suppressed a groan. And keep in mind, I <em>love</em> superheroes (I'm a huge Superman nerd, and loved <em>Unbreakable</em>)! But that just felt way too contrived for me.</p>
<p>I think that if the &quot;I Inspired Jerry Siegal To Co-Create Superman&quot; bit wasn't here, I'd probably be tentatively upvoting &#8212; I'd even be upvoting if the Jerry Siegal thing was just an easter-egg (casually mentioning his name is &quot;Jerry&quot; at some point). But the way you frame it in this interview gives that reveal a sense of gravitas that makes the entire article feel (to me) like a convoluted attempt to retroactively explain Superman was really an anomalous guy who saved Jerry Siegal's life. I'd much prefer this if he was inspired to become Superman by the comics themselves rather than inspiring the comics.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Take this with prescription grade salt</span> &#8212; maybe it's just my own personal biases getting in the way. I <em>will</em> say that, for what amounts to an X-Man skip, this is <em>very</em> well done. If we're going to have people with super-powers on the SCP-wiki (and I'm not at all opposed to that!), I'd be happy to see them demonstrate even just half of the amount of polish you're presenting here (prose-wise, image-wise, and structurally).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Anyway, to summarize: Tentatively a no-vote&#8230; but with an addendum that given the amount of polish and strong writing I'm seeing here, I'm definitely looking forward to seeing more from you despite not being able to upvote.</p>
<p>Good luck with the article!</p>

							</div>

								
					
